64-bit counterpart of ../tiger/: the same fork-tiger test programs, but
compiled with fork-tiger's tigerc -64, which emits bits64 C-- (target ...
wordsize 64 pointersize 64) instead of the default bits32. Generated with
regenerate-c--.sh; see that script's header for how to refresh a file.

Expected stdout/exit codes are the same Tiger programs computing the same
results, so output/*.1 and the *.0 stdin fixtures here are plain copies of
../tiger/'s (kept as a separate, self-contained folder, like every other
backend's tests/ subdirectory, not symlinks).

Status (2026-08-17): all 14 .c-- files elaborate cleanly (`qc -driver_elab`)
against every backend's metrics, i.e. they are well-formed bits64 C--.
Codegen (`qc -stop .s`) is split by backend:
  - -riscv64: all 14 emit assembly cleanly.
  - -alpha (the priority backend per CLAUDE.md - fork-tiger's own runtime
    is not yet 64-bit-clean either way, so this is not yet an end-to-end
    win): none codegen, surfacing real gaps in qc--'s own arch/alpha, not
    a problem with these files:
      - alloc_one: no registers to spill (even on hello) - alpha's
        register allocator/spiller can't handle these frames
      - "No acceptable widths for %mul/%quot on target 'alpha'; asked for
        64->64->64" - alpha's instruction selection only has 32-bit
        mul/quot
There is no tigermain-<arch>.o/stdlib-<arch>.a here yet (unlike ../tiger/'s
per-backend tigermain-*.o/stdlib-*.a) and no run-tiger64-<arch>.sh - those
need fork-tiger's runtime/stdlib built 64-bit-clean too, which is a
separate, larger piece of work than this directory's .c-- corpus.

While generating this directory, two real bugs were found and fixed in
fork-tiger's tigerc -64 output itself (both in frontend/frame.ml and
backend/codegen.ml - see their "claude:" comments there):
  - the GC descriptor tables' bare integer literals defaulted to the
    target's native word size (64), mismatching their declared bits32
    element type
  - the boolean-to-value widening after a comparison was hardcoded to
    %sx32 regardless of word size, mismatching bits64 temps
